{%- comment -%}
  Kept deliberately, and not yet called from anywhere.

  The size chart, for the product block that will open it.

  Theme Check reports this as `OrphanedSnippet`, and that report is correct:
  nothing calls it yet. It is a warning, not an error, and it stands until the
  file is wired up - which is the fix, rather than silencing the check.
{%- endcomment -%}

{% doc %}
  A size guide, opened from beside the option it explains.

  ## Where the content comes from

  Three sources, narrowest first, because a store selling both shoes and t-shirts
  needs different charts and a store selling one thing does not:

  1. **`product.metafields.custom.size_chart`** — a per-product chart. Accepts a
     page reference, rich text, or a metaobject with a `content` field, because
     merchants set this up all three ways depending on which app or admin flow
     they used.
  2. **A collection metafield** of the same name, so a whole category shares one
     chart without setting it on four hundred products.
  3. **The theme setting**, as the store-wide fallback.

  Renders nothing when all three are empty, so the trigger can never open an
  empty drawer.

  ## Placement

  The trigger sits next to the option it explains, not at the bottom of the form.
  A customer looks for a size guide while choosing a size, which is the moment
  they are least willing to hunt for it.

  Focus trapping, Escape and scroll locking come from `<modal-dialog>`, so this
  behaves like every other overlay in the theme rather than being a second
  implementation of the same thing.

  @param {product} [product] - For the metafield lookup
  @param {page} [page] - Overrides the resolution above
  @param {string} [option_name]
  @param {string} id - Unique per product form

  @example
    {% render 'size-chart-modal', product: product, option_name: option.name, id: section.id %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign chart_title = ''
  assign chart_content = ''

  comment
    A metafield can hold a page reference, rich text, or a metaobject. Merchants
    arrive at all three depending on which app or admin flow set it up, and a
    snippet that only handled one would look broken to two thirds of them.
  endcomment
  assign meta = product.metafields.custom.size_chart

  if meta == blank
    assign meta = collection.metafields.custom.size_chart
  endif

  if meta != blank
    if meta.type == 'page_reference' or meta.value.content != blank
      assign chart_title = meta.value.title
      assign chart_content = meta.value.content
    elsif meta.type == 'metaobject_reference'
      assign chart_title = meta.value.title
      assign chart_content = meta.value.content | default: meta.value.chart
    else
      assign chart_content = meta.value
    endif
  endif

  if page != blank
    assign chart_title = page.title
    assign chart_content = page.content
  endif

  if chart_content == blank and settings.size_chart_page != blank
    assign chart_title = settings.size_chart_page.title
    assign chart_content = settings.size_chart_page.content
  endif

  if chart_title == blank
    assign chart_title = 'products.size_chart.title' | t
  endif
-%}

{%- if chart_content != blank -%}
  <button
    type="button"
    class="size-chart__trigger button button--link"
    data-overlay-open="SizeChart-{{ id }}"
    aria-expanded="false"
    aria-controls="SizeChart-{{ id }}"
  >
    {% render 'icon', icon: 'ruler', size: 16 %}
    {{ 'products.size_chart.trigger' | t }}
    {%- if option_name != blank -%}
      <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.size_chart.for_option' | t: option: option_name }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </button>

  <modal-dialog id="SizeChart-{{ id }}" class="size-chart modal">
    <dialog class="modal__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ chart_title | escape }}">
      <div class="modal__panel size-chart__panel" data-ref="panel">
        <header class="size-chart__header">
          <h2 class="size-chart__title">{{ chart_title }}</h2>

          {%- liquid
            assign size_chart_close_label = 'products.size_chart.close' | t
            assign size_chart_close_id = 'SizeChartClose-' | append: id
          -%}
          {% render 'button',
             label: size_chart_close_label,
             id: size_chart_close_id,
             class: 'close-size-chart',
             icon: 'close',
             variant: 'primary',
             size: 'small',
             icon_only: true,
             attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}
        </header>

        <div class="modal__body rte" data-ref="body" data-lenis-prevent>
          {%- comment -%}
            The table gets its own scroll region with a tabindex, so a wide
            supplier chart scrolls inside the modal — and can be scrolled with a
            keyboard — rather than pushing the close button off screen.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div
            class="size-chart__table-wrapper"
            tabindex="0"
            role="region"
            aria-label="{{ chart_title | escape }}"
          >
            {{ chart_content }}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </dialog>
  </modal-dialog>
{%- endif -%}
